# Trivy findings this repository has looked at and decided against.
#
# Read by the `supply chain (trivy and sbom)` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml,
# which runs `trivy fs --scanners vuln,misconfig --severity HIGH,CRITICAL
# --exit-code 1`. Suppression is by rule id, and a bare id silences that rule
# everywhere, so each entry below carries the reason it is here and what would
# remove it. Anything not listed is a finding, and a finding fails the job.
#
# The file exists because the first run of that gate produced six findings and
# none of them was a defect in a deployed image. Two were real enough to fix
# instead (DS-0029 in .devcontainer/Dockerfile, and a comment on the ENV line
# DS-0031 flags in stremio-jellyfin/Dockerfile). The rest are below.

# DS-0002: last USER is root, or no USER at all.
#
# All four are containers that have to be root, and three of them are this
# repository's own test tooling rather than anything a NAS runs.
#
#   tests/e2e     writes to the mounted checkout on the runner and reads
#                 /var/run/docker.sock to reach the daemon. Both are root-owned
#                 on the runner; a non-root user would fail at the first write
#                 or the first docker call, which is what these images are for.
#   tests/mutation  runs the containerised mutation oracle, which mutates files
#                 in its own copy of the tree.
#   tests/toolkit   the pytest and kcov image, same shape: it writes reports
#                 into a mounted directory.
#   duc-service     a real deployed image, and the one worth revisiting. nginx
#                 binds port 80 inside it and startup.sh drives dpkg-installed
#                 cron; both want root. The fix is setcap on the nginx binary
#                 plus dropping privileges in startup.sh, which changes how a
#                 live service starts. Worth doing deliberately, with the duc
#                 container healthy in front of you, not as a side effect of
#                 adding a scanner.
DS-0002
